matching algorithms to predict

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.560
the failure of an asset,
which is not really accurate

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:11.670
and can be unreliable
in many instances.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:15.630
The solution that we propose
is true predictive maintenance.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:20.580
Both the end user and also the
independent software vendor

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:25.410
can develop ML models from
scratch, or taking something

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.760
and amplifying the accuracy
for some of those models.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:32.510
This is for any
asset that they have.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.830
And they can
incorporate this model

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:36.720
into their on-prem application.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:43.150
Or if the app lies outside of
the firewall, in many cases,

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:47.070
especially in
factories, then you

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:50.380
can do callbacks to the
server to get new models.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:53.340
So if it's within the
firewall, then the model

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:58.920
has to be on a gateway
device or some sort of device

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.520
right next to the asset.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.460
But if there is an
opportunity to put the model

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:08.430
outside of the firewall, then
you have a little more freedom

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:13.980
to go back and forth, do
callbacks to the cloud.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:18.690
So in this case, we
used a specific kind

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:22.190
of RNN, which stands for
Recurrent Neural Network called

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:25.920
LSTM, which is Long
Short-Term Memory.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.727
That's what it stands for.

00:16:27.727 --> 00:16:29.310
That's the model
that we started with.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.800
And then we've built a
predictive maintenance

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.600
for this particular
application, which I'm

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.920
going to demo in a few seconds.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.200
And so once you build the
model, then it's followed--

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.790
evaluating the model.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.570
So use the test data
to test the model.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:49.360
And the 80/20 rule is that
80% of the data that you've

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:51.360
gathered you can use
to train the model.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.520
And 20% of the data can
be used to test the model.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:57.990
So once the model is
hypertuned, then it's

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.341
ready for deployment
and prediction.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.510
So let me provide
you with some context

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.140
before I jump into the demo.

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:12.970
So we're going to
take a real use case

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:15.700
and demonstrate
machine learning.

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:19.750
As you know, a typical oil
rig has about thousands

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:22.240
of sensors and control
system modules,

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.970
acquiring telemetry
and process data.

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:27.230
These are complex assets.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:30.100
And these assets are located
in the middle of nowhere.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:35.380
And some of these assets, they
don't have good connectivity

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:36.400
to the cloud.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:40.420
So the models may have
to reside at the Edge.

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:44.305
So as a side note, in
this particular demo,

00:17:44.305 --> 00:17:47.620
we're going to assume
that the model is running

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.470
right next to the asset itself.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:58.420
So with that in mind, we can
go and see some of the things

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:01.670
that we have done
on the demo site.

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:05.980
So again, now just
to set the context,

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:10.240
this is an example of
the complexities involved

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:13.810
in capturing and analyzing
data from connected devices.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:19.530
So let's switch
over to the demo.

00:18:24.971 --> 00:18:25.470
There we go.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:35.850
And again, this is the
fictitious upstream oil and gas

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:36.600
manufacturer.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:39.150
So here, they have
a bunch of assets.

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:43.590
When you double click on
one of those locations,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:51.210
as you can tell, there's
multiple sensors on these rigs.

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:56.290
And you can drill into the
specifics of each of those.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:19:02.700
So let me switch over
to the demo to show you

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.740
exactly what I mean by that.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:05.260
There we go.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:26.600
So when you click
one of these sensors,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:30.280
it'll show you the
telemetry data coming

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:32.620
from those different sensors.

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:36.700
So in this case, the
operational dashboard

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.940
reflects the real
time machines data.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:45.280
So this could be the tension,
the pressure, temperature,

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:49.570
the viscosity of the oil.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:54.580
And here's a simple architecture
of the predictive maintenance

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:56.270
application itself.

00:19:56.270 --> 00:20:00.070
So the data is gathered
from these different rigs.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:05.440
And Cloud IoT Core publishes
the data to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:08.530
So once the machine data has
been gathered through a gateway

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:11.800
device, it gets published
to the Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:14.170
And Google IoT Core,
once it receives it--

00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:18.100
of course, with the time stamp--

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:21.310
it publishes to the Pub/Sub.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:26.920
And from there, the data flows
to the data flow as an event.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:31.180
And we make DNN regular serve
Cloud ML call from the data

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:32.110
flow.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:37.270
And again, the data is posted
on the BigQuery in parallel.

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:39.280
And also the data
is sent in real time

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.710
to the Cloud ML Engine.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:45.580
So the predictive
maintenance model

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:48.370
is being built in real time.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:52.330
Then once it's
built, then you can

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:58.250
deploy it, and start predicting
right then and there.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:04.120
So once you deploy the model,
once you start the prediction,

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.360
either you can write it
to a BigQuery itself,

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:11.560
or otherwise, you can show it
in real time on a visualization

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:12.380
engine.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.800
So this is a very
simple architecture

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:16.457
of predictive maintenance.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:25.020
So this is a bunch
of sensor data.

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.730
So we are monitoring the device
health with incoming time

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:30.560
series data here.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:33.080
And we can also easily
and accurately determine

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.940
the predictive remaining life.

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:40.460
So if we go back to the
previous slide, the red bar

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:44.570
that you see, it
shows exactly what's

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:47.600
the predicted cycle remaining.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:53.970
In this case, zero, which means
that the asset is failing.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.090
So you can actually
get this in real time.

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:00.040
So let's say that if a
predictive lifetime of an asset

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:05.345
is 172 days, then you know that
within 172 days, plus or minus

00:22:05.345 --> 00:22:08.330
whatever their rate is,
the asset will fail.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:13.130
So you can plan a planned
down time, rather than

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:14.981
an unplanned downtime,
which costs millions

00:22:14.981 --> 00:22:15.980
and millions of dollars.

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.980
So the predictive accuracy
is very important.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:26.140
So some of the applications
that I talked about previously,

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:29.210
the predictive applications
that you see in the market, some

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:32.420
of them are not that
accurate, which is not

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:33.920
the way it's supposed to be.

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:37.720
So when you build the model
using the ML Engine, especially

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.055
the RNN models that
I was talking about,

00:22:40.055 --> 00:22:46.590
you can actually hypertune it
to decrease the error rate.

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:50.850
So when you see that
green line pointing down,

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:54.020
which means the
hypertuning is happening,

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:57.040
and we are increasing the
accuracy of the model.

