00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:19.560
Hello, welcome back everyone.

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:22.060
So we have a list of questions.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:24.630
Let me go through it one by one.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.000
So how does Google
help enterprises

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.610
build predictive
maintenance solutions?

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:34.140
How does an enterprise go
from building a POC/pilot

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:36.775
to building a live
production deployment?

00:31:36.775 --> 00:31:37.650
It's a good question.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:43.440
So like I said before,
the best place to start

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:48.480
is once you understand the
business problem very well,

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:50.250
then you need data.

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:55.275
So from my experience
at least, say

00:31:55.275 --> 00:31:58.290
I'm putting some context
to this question.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:03.720
Let's say if it's an oil
refinery or some sort

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:05.970
of a manufacturing plant,
either process or discrete,

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:08.420
it doesn't matter,
most of those datas

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:10.710
are stored in historian on-prem.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:14.010
Could be a level 2 historian,
a level 3 historian.

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:19.200
In some cases, they do store
it in a private data center.

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:21.810
But the data doesn't
leave the premises.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.630
In those instances,
what I would recommend

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:27.840
is, first, normalize the data.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.990
What I mean by normalizing is
it could be a number of formats.

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:35.370
I hear that there is about
150 protocols out there

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:40.290
like Modbus DMB3,
BACnet, you name it.

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:43.770
So in the batch
processing world at least,

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:47.700
I've seen OPC and OPCU8,
quite famous protocols.

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:50.430
So normalizing it in
one of those formats,

00:32:50.430 --> 00:32:54.870
and then taking the normalized
data and cleaning it up--

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:58.990
basically, doing the ETL
transformation on the data.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:02.460
So for example, you don't
have to send every single bits

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:05.050
and bytes out of the historian.

00:33:05.050 --> 00:33:08.940
You normalize doing
average temperature

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:10.300
over a particular window.

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:10.800
Right?

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:14.520
So whatever you can do
to clean up the data,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.940
and then you can
take that clean data

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:22.200
and start doing
machine learning.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:25.170
So one way to start
machine learning

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:28.500
is, if you're not familiar
with some of these models,

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.360
there is hundreds of
thousands of models

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:32.640
already out there open-source.

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:36.270
So of course, Google
has contributed to many

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:38.340
of those models already.

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:41.130
You can go to
TensorFlow or Cafe,

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:43.020
or there are so many
other services out there

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:46.419
where you can take one
of these models that

00:33:46.419 --> 00:33:48.210
can do a pretty good
predictive maintenance

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.370
and start with that.

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:53.490
Now with the data and
with a decent model,

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:55.350
you can start building.

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.870
ML Engine, one of
the Google services,

00:33:57.870 --> 00:34:01.750
can be used to actually train
that model, make it better.

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:04.260
And then you have
to make a decision

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:09.030
whether to include that model
as part of an application,

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:14.850
or to have an app,
have a model separate,

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:17.370
and do some sort
of a connection.

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:21.360
So if you have an asset
manager or an asset

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:23.310
predictive
maintenance, if you're

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:25.446
running some sort of an
application like that,

00:34:25.446 --> 00:34:26.820
then I would
definitely recommend

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:29.340
that model stays separate
from your application,

00:34:29.340 --> 00:34:31.870
because you may
have many assets.

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:34.889
So you can build models
for individual assets--

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:39.600
could be turbines, could
be steam engine or whatnot.

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:43.710
So that's the best
place to start.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:47.730
And we have end to end
services to do that right now

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:49.050
on Google Cloud.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.780
As a matter of fact,
there's a multiple webinars

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:55.710
that's already been done on
YouTube that you can go take

00:34:55.710 --> 00:34:57.870
a look at to start this work.

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.255
So good luck with that.

00:34:59.255 --> 00:35:02.690
The second question is,
what kind of protocols

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.250
does IoT Core support?

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:10.650
So right now, we
support MQDT and HTTP.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:13.710
But in the future, we
may support protocols

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:15.840
like AMQP and CoApps.

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:23.520
So any device that can
communicate MQDT or HTTP

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:25.960
can connect to IoT Core.

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:28.500
And IoT Core is a
universal endpoint.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:31.350
So what works, doesn't matter
where your devices are,

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:33.120
could be anywhere
in the world as

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:36.150
long as you can talk
to those protocol,

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:40.020
you can communicate
to Cloud IoT Core.

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:42.930
So can you list some of
the customers or platforms?

00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:47.370
Yes, so in my presentation,
I've listed some examples.

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:50.580
Schlumberger is one,
Oden Technologies.

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:53.550
And top of that, we have
FogHorn, which is our partner.

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.780
They built that IoT platform
on top of GCP as well.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:04.480
And we also have our Aila.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:10.590
They've also recently started
supporting or integrating

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:13.470
their solution with
IoT Core Service.

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:16.750
So your seeing a lot of
IoT platform providers

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:19.430
supporting that.

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:22.490
So what if I don't
have any training data?

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:26.990
Can I gather it in real time?

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:27.890
Yes.

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:34.880
So of course, any time we
talk about machine learning,

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:37.450
clean data and a
lot of data is good.

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:38.870
It's always the case.

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:43.221
But we do have certain
pre-built APIs.

00:36:43.221 --> 00:36:45.470
Like, for example, in many
of the industrial use cases

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:53.010
I've seen, the visual
API, that comes in handy.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:55.910
So it's already pre-built model.

00:36:55.910 --> 00:36:58.520
So you can start with maybe
hundreds and thousands

00:36:58.520 --> 00:36:59.519
of images.

00:36:59.519 --> 00:37:01.310
Let's say that if you
don't have thousands,

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:03.644
you can start with 100 images.

00:37:03.644 --> 00:37:05.060
And you can still
train that model

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:08.990
for a certain application.

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:14.150
Let's say that if you want to do
some sort of a thermal imaging

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:17.060
on something and
predict the result,

00:37:17.060 --> 00:37:19.520
then you can use a video
intelligence for it.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:23.810
Of course, we also
have NLP, which

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:26.660
is a natural language
processing API.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:33.770
Again, if you wanted to
read a bunch of data points,

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:38.090
could be a sentence, or it could
be a Twitter feed or whatnot--

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:41.300
and if you wanted to
derive emotions from that,

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:42.500
you can certainly do that.

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:45.060
So for that, you don't
need a ton of data.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:47.110
And you can use real time
data that's coming into

00:37:47.110 --> 00:37:52.250
to do machine learning on that.

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:55.190
And we also have another
service called AutoML.

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:58.660
So if you're not sure
what model to pick,

00:37:58.660 --> 00:38:01.670
you don't have
high quality data,

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:04.740
then AutoML is
another way to go.

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:09.470
So with the limited
data, it can actually

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:12.470
recommend you a
pretty good model

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:18.860
where the accuracy is north of
70% at least in most use cases.

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:23.420
And then you can kind
of hypertune from there.

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:26.510
The next one is,
does Google Cloud

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:30.200
offer out of the box models
for image recognition

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:31.010
and classification?

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:31.850
Yes.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:35.660
So the visual API that
I was talking about

00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:38.390
can be used for image
classification and image

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:40.100
recognition.

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:44.960
And a lot of the industrial
use cases I've seen

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:46.130
have a bunch of labels.

00:38:46.130 --> 00:38:47.960
And they wanted
to be able to name

00:38:47.960 --> 00:38:50.630
those labels, what those are.

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:57.132
So in those cases again, you can
use the visual API to do that.

00:38:57.132 --> 00:38:59.090
And of course, there's
a lot of other use cases

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:02.840
where a pre-built model
will come in handy in those

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:03.677
instances.

00:39:03.677 --> 00:39:05.510
You can refer to one
of the pre-built models

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:08.720
that we're already marketing,
or you can actually

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:13.460
find something from the open
source community as well.

00:39:13.460 --> 00:39:18.160
And use the ML
Engine or AutoML too

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:20.780
to build and train the model.

00:39:20.780 --> 00:39:23.926
So where can I go to try
out the IoT Services?

00:39:23.926 --> 00:39:24.800
It's a good question.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:31.430
So cloud.google.com/iot-core,
that's one of the IoT services

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:32.530
that we have.

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:35.270
And that you can actually
go to cloud.Google.com

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:36.780
to do a free trial.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:41.040
So on the top right hand corner
you would see a Launch button.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:44.395
Once you launch, it will ask
you for your Google account.

00:39:44.395 --> 00:39:47.990
So if you don't have one,
you can create one there.

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:51.245
And then you can take advantage
of some of the cloud services.

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:57.090
So the next question
is related to pricing.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:40:01.880
So we do offer the first
250 MB free per month

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:05.730
for the IoT Core Service.

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:08.030
And we have a very
competitive pricing model

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:14.900
and a simple pricing model
for you to understand.

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:18.740
The next one is, can I
do real time analytics

00:40:18.740 --> 00:40:21.530
on telemetry data
received from equipment?

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:22.310
Yes.

00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:26.270
So we can do both real time just
streaming analytics and also

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:28.310
batch analytics.

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:30.830
The way we
differentiate is right

00:40:30.830 --> 00:40:33.270
now to do those
different things,

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:35.790
you may have to write
additional line of codes,

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:39.110
or you may have to use
different tools altogether.

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:43.370
So in Google Cloud's
case, we have a product

00:40:43.370 --> 00:40:48.410
called Data Flow, which
is fully managed service

00:40:48.410 --> 00:40:50.600
where you can do
e-tail transformation

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:54.980
and also do analytics using
BigQuery in real time.

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:59.150
And don't have to write
additional line of codes

00:40:59.150 --> 00:41:05.700
for streaming analytics
and batch analytics.

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:10.760
And it's fairly easy to
orchestrate a ETL pipeline

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.180
and go from there.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:15.890
So again, there is ton of
documentation resources

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:19.690
out there, both in video format
and also in documentation

00:41:19.690 --> 00:41:21.820
format for you to go look at.

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:25.750
So I would highly
recommend looking

00:41:25.750 --> 00:41:30.010
at some of the next videos
or the Google IO videos

00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:32.440
that we have done on that.

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:36.400
The last question is,
I don't know Python.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:39.040
Can I do ML in any
other language?

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:39.850
Yes.

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:42.130
So we do support
multiple languages.

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:45.670
We do support R, we
support of course, Python,

00:41:45.670 --> 00:41:48.740
and C++ in certain areas.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:54.480
So there are multiple
ways that you can program

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:56.860
machine learning models.

00:41:56.860 --> 00:42:02.410
And I don't know exactly how
many languages we support.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:06.560
But that is something that
you can refer to our ML Engine

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:09.670
website to gather the details.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:12.920
Again, the documentation
is pretty good there.

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:16.210
So with that, thank
you for your time.

00:42:16.210 --> 00:42:21.640
And the next session
that we have is on AI.

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:27.790
So I highly recommend that you
stick around to watch that.

00:42:27.790 --> 00:42:32.640
